{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790721990,"nonce":"CS8tozlION82RZMMahLpsu793Oc=","title":"Reduce native worker wake cost for short frame barriers","message":"An Ash AOT FixedThreadPool(4) control with no work inside jobs spends about 26-30 ms on 20 frame barriers when ASH_WORKERS=4. The same bytecode with ASH_WORKERS=0 takes about 0.02 ms for one empty job per frame or 0.2 ms for 16 empty jobs per frame. The pool is created and warmed before the timer. The timed loop allocates about 0.04 MB and triggers no GC collections. ASH_FIBER_TRACE shows park-main to wake/resume-main gaps often around 1-3 ms even for an empty job. Measurements are from a local macOS arm64 AOT build and have run-to-run noise; the consistent order-of-magnitude gap warrants profiling the cross-thread wake path.\n\nMinimal Haxe shape:\n\n    var pool = new sys.thread.FixedThreadPool(4);\n    var done = new sys.thread.Lock();\n    for (_ in 0...4) pool.run(() -\u003e done.release());\n    for (_ in 0...4) done.wait();\n    var start = Sys.time();\n    for (_ in 0...20) {\n        for (_ in 0...16) pool.run(() -\u003e done.release());\n        for (_ in 0...16) done.wait();\n    }\n    Sys.println((Sys.time() - start) * 1000);\n    pool.shutdown();\n\nCompile to AOT, then compare ASH_WORKERS=0 and ASH_WORKERS=4. Also try one job per frame: the native-worker cost remains near the same size, indicating barrier latency more than closure count. A compute-only control with large NativeArray jobs and the same pool scales from roughly 0.9 s serial to 0.13 s with four native workers, so the worker pool can parallelize substantial work.\n\nThis is separate from hybrid mode leaving cold Thread.create roots on the interpreter scheduler (issue d5bc1aa5739f0247bceef000aa6baa5cdb8d796de72030d331e304fb02d8dbdf). ChimpMonk's island task probe also allocates about 39 MB over 320 small world steps, making both frame barriers and allocation throughput relevant. Investigate scheduler wake/park latency and whether a reusable batch completion primitive can avoid repeated main-worker round trips for short tasks.","files":null}]}